WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.542
Clickjacking at a high level is a user-interface redress risk where a user
may be tricked into interacting with an application in an unintended
embedding context.

2
00:00:08.542 --> 00:00:12.221
Framing controls help the browser decide where a page may be embedded.

3
00:00:12.221 --> 00:00:19.828
Modern policies often use CSP frame-ancestors concepts, while legacy
frame-control headers may still appear in older compatibility designs.

4
00:00:19.828 --> 00:00:22.821
The durable lesson is to make embedding policy explicit.

5
00:00:22.821 --> 00:00:25.377
Not every page should have the same framing rule.

6
00:00:25.377 --> 00:00:28.183
Many applications should prevent framing entirely.

7
00:00:28.183 --> 00:00:34.855
Some dashboards, partner portals, reporting surfaces, or embedded widgets
may need controlled embedding by expected sites.

8
00:00:34.855 --> 00:00:40.092
Internal tools may need even stricter treatment because their users often
have powerful sessions.

9
00:00:40.092 --> 00:00:45.579
The right policy depends on business needs, user workflows, and which
pages perform sensitive actions.

10
00:00:45.579 --> 00:00:49.445
Framing controls should be tested across expected embedding scenarios.

11
00:00:49.445 --> 00:00:53.935
A policy that blocks a legitimate partner integration may be removed under
pressure.

12
00:00:53.935 --> 00:00:58.112
A policy that allows broad embedding may fail to protect sensitive
interfaces.

13
00:00:58.112 --> 00:01:05.906
Teams should document where embedding is expected, which origins are
allowed, which pages should never be framed, and how exceptions are
approved.

14
00:01:05.906 --> 00:01:09.024
This keeps security policy aligned with product behavior.

15
00:01:09.024 --> 00:01:12.079
Framing headers are not a complete access-control model.

16
00:01:12.079 --> 00:01:18.252
They do not replace authentication, authorization, CSRF protection, or
confirmation steps for high-risk actions.

17
00:01:18.252 --> 00:01:21.993
They reduce a browser-side risk by controlling presentation context.

18
00:01:21.993 --> 00:01:31.720
A secure design combines framing policy with server-side permission
checks, safe session behavior, clear UI flows, and monitoring for unusual
administrative or sensitive activity.
